import { JsonReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> JsonReaderCopy = JsonReaderCopy {
  summary: "Read DecodeError's path and kind to explain where an API-data check failed. This example identifies which person's name in people caused the problem.",
  taskTitle: "Locate a name that could not be read",
  purpose: "Extract string names from an API's people array. If a name has the wrong type or is missing, give the caller enough information to find the problem. An empty array is a valid result.",
  typescriptBefore: "The TypeScript version receives JSON.parse's result as unknown, then checks the object, the people field, the array, and each person's name. The for loop returns at the first problem and builds the location and reason there. All supplied strings are syntactically valid JSON; this comparison focuses on field and type checks.",
  benefit: "Combining field and array in Seseragi carries the failed location in DecodeError. The name check stays reusable without building display messages, while decodeErrorText handles the presentation of locations and reasons in one place.",
  setup: "json and text are aliases for the imported modules. fn segment value: json.JsonPathSegment -> String defines an ordinary function: the colon gives value's type, and -> gives the return type. Calls put spaces between a function and its arguments, as in segment item.",
  after: [
    "The annotation in let stringDecoder: json.Decoder<String> = decodeJson selects the JSON string checker. Decoder<String> is a function receiving Json and returning either Left with a failure reason or Right with a checked String. nameDecoder checks name; peopleDecoder checks the names within the people array.",
    "report uses match to select a branch for each outcome. Left _ from parse ignores the syntax-error detail; Right value passes the parsed value to peopleDecoder. Only its Left error goes to the formatter. Right names becomes a JSON string of checked names.",
    "error.path contains JsonField names and JsonIndex positions in root-to-leaf order. segment matches each one to make text such as .name or [1]. The expression [segment item | item <- path] formats each segment, and text.join joins them after a leading $. Here show converts only the integer index to text.",
    "error.kind describes the reason. kindText matches ExpectedJsonType to obtain the expected type, MissingJsonField to obtain the absent name, and the other reasons similarly. A false second name produces $.people[1].name | expected JsonString. A missing second name produces $.people[1] | missing name.",
    "Only main is an effect function for printing. do runs println calls in order, and _ <- discards each earlier print result. Unit means there is no useful return value; with Console fails ConsoleError declares console access and possible printing failure. The JSON checks themselves are ordinary functions.",
  ],
  details: [
    "A missing field's name is in kind. Its path therefore ends at the parent, people[1], when name is absent. The name segment is added when name exists but checking its value fails. An empty path means the root, displayed as $ here.",
    "array stops at the first failed element. The null name after the false name is not checked, so this is not a list of every problem. The $, .name, and [1] notation is a display convention for the sample's simple field names, not an implementation of the general JSONPath standard.",
    "You can read DecodeError.path and .kind, but direct DecodeError { ... } construction and a named struct pattern are private. DecodeError, DecodeErrorKind, JsonReadError, JsonParseError, and JsonPathSegment also have no Eq, Show, or Debug instances. Instead of show error, build a message from the public fields and alternatives as this example does.",
  ],
}
